# snipe/snipe-it acceptance-reminder mail over GET. The reminder action is
# registered as Route::get('reports/unaccepted_assets/{acceptanceId}/
# sent_reminder', [ReportsController::class, 'sentAssetAcceptanceReminder']),
# and the handler dispatched a CheckoutAssetNotification mail through the
# Notification facade. Laravel's VerifyCsrfToken middleware only validates the
# state-changing verbs, so a GET route is outside CSRF protection by
# construction: any page a signed-in operator visits could trigger the mail
# (cross-site request forgery, CWE-352; GHSA-33vj-r6p6-x4p8).
#
# The fixed revision (6d55d782, "Set resend acceptance to POST") changes exactly one
# discriminating thing: the registration becomes Route::post('reports/
# unaccepted_assets/sent_reminder', ...), which brings the route under the web
# group's token middleware, and the report view swaps the reminder <a href>
# for a <form method="post"> carrying @csrf. Inside the controller the fixed revision
# only re-reads the identifier from $request->input('acceptance_id') and adds
# \Log::debug calls -- no validation of any token appears in the handler.
#
# ATTENTION, csrf-decision completeness, the route-wiring form of the family
# at ranks 1567 (phpBB form key), 1581 (pkp FormValidatorCSRF), 1592
# (polarlearn oauth state) and 1601 (prefect csrf client). The discriminator
# is the HTTP verb at the route registration, which lives in routes/web.php
# while the state change lives in a handler reached only through the string
# literal inside the [Controller::class, 'method'] array -- there is no
# route-table resolution joining the two, so no binding can ask "is the
# handler that changes state registered on a safe verb". Keying on the verb
# alone labels every legitimate GET route in the application. The 1380
# counterweight fails: the fixed revision leaves no API-documented control call inside
# the guarded method, only logging and a different parameter source.
#
# Measured on the real code, three --cache off runs per side over
# app/Http/Controllers + routes (98 files): 324 findings at the vulnerable
# revision and 324 at the fixed revision, the same three rows touching the pair's own
# files at both -- VYQL-RF-043 on the sibling deleteAssetAcceptance's
# $acceptance->delete() (ReportsController.php:1098 -> 1108) and on the two
# Route::delete registrations in web.php -- none on the reminder route or its
# handler. vyql match reports identical 73-label sets at both revisions with
# no source label at either: the vulnerable $acceptanceId parameter and the
# patched $request->input('acceptance_id') are both unlabelled. A candidate
# sink binding on the notification dispatch (callee.path ~= "Notification.send"
# / "Notification.locale.send") is measured and dropped: it adds exactly two
# findings and fires identically at both revisions (324 -> 327 -> 327), zero
# discrimination, because the dispatch is present unchanged on both sides.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-csrf-decision

test "rank1763: the reminder action is registered on GET and dispatches the mail"
